WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:04.760
I'm Jenna Fisher.

2
00:00:04.940 --> 00:00:06.060
And I'm Angela Kinsey.

3
00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:07.340
We were on The Office together.

4
00:00:07.580 --> 00:00:08.600
And we're best friends.

5
00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:12.420
And now we're doing the Ultimate Office rewatch podcast just for you.

6
00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:19.400
Each week, we will break down an episode of The Office and give exclusive behind-the-scenes stories that only two people who were there can tell you.

7
00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:20.560
We're The Office Ladies.

8
00:00:23.640 --> 00:00:24.940
Oh, hi, everyone.

9
00:00:25.840 --> 00:00:32.680
Today, we will be discussing Michael's last dundies in our first new recording space.

10
00:00:33.020 --> 00:00:35.420
Yes, we are renting the cutest space.

11
00:00:35.420 --> 00:00:36.180
Hi, Sam, Cassie.

12
00:00:36.280 --> 00:00:36.640
Say hello.

13
00:00:36.820 --> 00:00:37.380
We're all here.

14
00:00:37.460 --> 00:00:37.960
Hi, everyone.

15
00:00:38.340 --> 00:00:38.620
Hi.

16
00:00:39.280 --> 00:00:40.180
They're behind.

17
00:00:40.620 --> 00:00:41.840
It's kind of like a shower curtain.

18
00:00:42.580 --> 00:00:44.340
Yeah, we can see them.

19
00:00:44.820 --> 00:00:47.360
This studio is so adorable.

20
00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:48.560
I have a coaster.

21
00:00:48.980 --> 00:00:49.220
You do.

22
00:00:49.500 --> 00:00:52.320
Like some of the personal touches in the bathroom.

23
00:00:52.840 --> 00:00:55.820
It's clear that the owners have put up their children's artwork.

24
00:00:56.020 --> 00:00:56.560
It's adorable.

25
00:00:56.920 --> 00:00:58.740
Which is so my thing.

26
00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:01.620
And Jenna, how would you say the temperature is in here?

27
00:01:01.940 --> 00:01:04.200
Sam, the temperature is just temperature.

28
00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:06.320
It's neither hot nor cold.

29
00:01:06.760 --> 00:01:07.320
Yes.

30
00:01:07.480 --> 00:01:11.180
And the table we're sitting at was the owner's grandmother's table.

31
00:01:11.180 --> 00:01:13.280
And it's the perfect height for me.

32
00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:16.700
Maybe she was petite in stature because I don't need my footstool.

33
00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:18.100
It's very exciting.

34
00:01:18.280 --> 00:01:18.860
It's very exciting.

35
00:01:19.620 --> 00:01:29.020
So today, in our new space, we will be discussing Season 7, Episode 21, written by Mindy Kaling and directed by Mindy Kaling.

36
00:01:29.240 --> 00:01:30.320
Would you like a summary?

37
00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:31.640
I can't wait for it.

38
00:01:31.640 --> 00:01:32.720
I'm going to be honest with you.

39
00:01:32.780 --> 00:01:34.040
I struggled with this summary.

40
00:01:34.300 --> 00:01:34.540
Oh.

41
00:01:34.760 --> 00:01:36.760
It seems like the title says it all.

42
00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:37.320
Mm-hmm.

43
00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:38.940
So how do you expand on that?

44
00:01:39.040 --> 00:01:39.860
Here's what I got.

45
00:01:40.140 --> 00:01:42.020
Oh, well, you're really setting me up.

46
00:01:42.340 --> 00:01:43.480
To be disappointed.

47
00:01:43.800 --> 00:01:44.180
To be blown away.

48
00:01:44.300 --> 00:01:44.680
All right.

49
00:01:45.300 --> 00:01:48.060
It is time for Michael's last Dundies.

50
00:01:48.460 --> 00:01:54.800
Michael taps D'Angelo to co-host his final Dundie Awards, but D'Angelo has massive stage fright.

51
00:01:54.800 --> 00:01:58.620
And Michael worries about his legacy continuing after he's gone.

52
00:01:59.340 --> 00:02:07.400
Meanwhile, Erin's personal life takes a turn when she follows Pam's advice, and everyone gives Michael a special send-off.

53
00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:09.180
I would add one thing to that.

54
00:02:09.300 --> 00:02:09.820
What is it?

55
00:02:09.960 --> 00:02:11.200
Dwight's heart is broken.

56
00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:12.380
Mm, you're right.

57
00:02:12.700 --> 00:02:14.240
That is a major plot point.

58
00:02:14.440 --> 00:02:16.200
See, I struggled with the summary.

59
00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:17.540
I told you.

60
00:02:19.080 --> 00:02:19.780
All right.

61
00:02:19.960 --> 00:02:21.100
Fast fact number one.

62
00:02:21.300 --> 00:02:24.780
Like I said in the beginning, this was written and directed by Mindy Kaling.

63
00:02:25.180 --> 00:02:30.680
You know, Mindy wrote the first Dundies episode, and this time around, she both wrote and directed the episode.

64
00:02:30.820 --> 00:02:31.320
Very exciting.

65
00:02:31.640 --> 00:02:31.980
Yes.

66
00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:33.360
And we reached out to Mindy.

67
00:02:33.480 --> 00:02:34.520
She's always so gracious.

68
00:02:34.520 --> 00:02:36.380
She always gets back to us right away.

69
00:02:36.600 --> 00:02:40.480
She got us audio clips a day earlier than our deadline.

70
00:02:40.740 --> 00:02:40.940
Yeah.

71
00:02:41.020 --> 00:02:42.960
She wrote back and said, you guys, let me rewatch it.

72
00:02:43.060 --> 00:02:44.120
I'll send you audio clips.

73
00:02:44.260 --> 00:02:45.860
We're going to sprinkle them throughout the episode.

74
00:02:45.860 --> 00:02:52.420
Well, first off, we asked her a bunch of questions about getting to write and direct this episode, and here's what she had to say.

75
00:02:52.660 --> 00:02:53.800
Hey, Jenna and Angela.

76
00:02:54.760 --> 00:02:58.280
Thanks for these incredibly thoughtful questions about Michael's last Dundies.

77
00:02:58.500 --> 00:03:06.360
So I have to admit to you guys that I was feeling really vulnerable about this particular episode and revisiting it and watching it.

78
00:03:06.880 --> 00:03:12.460
And I tried to pinpoint why that is, because I have nothing but joyous memories of working on the show.

79
00:03:12.580 --> 00:03:15.000
And I think the answer is because I directed this one.

80
00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:22.300
And I was so nervous directing the show because I loved writing for the show.

81
00:03:22.440 --> 00:03:29.220
And to this day, I think I've only directed two episodes of television, and they were both from The Office 16 years ago.

82
00:03:29.360 --> 00:03:30.680
So I loved directing.

83
00:03:30.860 --> 00:03:35.980
I think it was awesome that Greg and Howard let me write and direct an episode of TV.

84
00:03:35.980 --> 00:03:40.780
In fact, it was their suggestion that I did it because I was too scared to volunteer.

85
00:03:41.420 --> 00:03:46.800
I think Greg chose for me to direct this episode because it was a bookend with the first Dundies episode.

86
00:03:46.960 --> 00:03:48.820
But I don't think we ever spoke about that.

87
00:03:49.040 --> 00:03:58.760
Greg is not, like, so sentimental that he would have been like, do you remember six years ago or seven years ago when you wrote The Dundies and now we've come full circle?

88
00:03:58.760 --> 00:04:02.380
I mean, I wish he was that way, but he's just not.

89
00:04:03.360 --> 00:04:04.320
It's very funny.

90
00:04:04.460 --> 00:04:10.000
I had wondered how she got assigned to this episode since she had written the first one.

91
00:04:10.120 --> 00:04:11.960
I thought maybe it was intentional.

92
00:04:12.400 --> 00:04:12.560
Yeah.

93
00:04:13.000 --> 00:04:19.460
We also asked Mindy, what were some of the biggest differences between writing the first Dundies and then writing and directing this one?

94
00:04:19.560 --> 00:04:20.240
Here's what she said.

95
00:04:20.240 --> 00:04:30.760
I think the biggest thing was how much more enjoyable it was for me to have written the first Dundies than it was to write and direct this one from season seven,

96
00:04:30.900 --> 00:04:37.460
which seems crazy because I was directing Carell and Will Ferrell and, you know, obviously our amazing caSt.

97
00:04:37.620 --> 00:04:42.880
I was directing a script I had written, which is like everybody's dream is to direct their own words.

98
00:04:42.880 --> 00:04:44.740
And I just remember being terrified.

99
00:04:45.360 --> 00:04:53.360
Um, and I'll tell you what really terrified me and this is going to sound ridiculous, but like making sure I didn't sleep through my alarm because when you're a director,

100
00:04:53.700 --> 00:04:55.160
you have to be on time.

101
00:04:55.260 --> 00:05:02.240
It's not like an actor where you can kind of be eight minutes late and the hair and makeup team can kind of make up the time and you're like,

102
00:05:02.300 --> 00:05:04.000
it is such a bad look if you're not there.

103
00:05:04.140 --> 00:05:05.140
So I was really terrified.

104
00:05:05.140 --> 00:05:10.920
Um, I also had the sense that this was an episode that was, you know,

105
00:05:11.040 --> 00:05:16.500
sort of important in the emotional arc of the series where Michael would be leaving and it was like our beloved Steve.

106
00:05:16.960 --> 00:05:28.540
And it was, I think that we wrote the script, you know, it with the Dundies, it's always one of those ones where you have to come up with these like jokey jokes.

107
00:05:28.540 --> 00:05:34.520
And by jokey jokes, I mean, the names of the Dundee Awards, it's not just like a joke from the office, it's like an extra joke.

108
00:05:34.580 --> 00:05:37.280
It has to be like something that a character on the show would come up with.

109
00:05:37.400 --> 00:05:40.100
So it can be funny, but not like so clever.

110
00:05:40.340 --> 00:05:42.360
So I remember that being challenging.

111
00:05:42.760 --> 00:05:47.100
I mean, I have to imagine getting to pitch Dundee Award jokes is so much fun.

112
00:05:47.800 --> 00:05:49.500
I'm sure that took up a whole night.

113
00:05:49.740 --> 00:05:50.120
Right.

114
00:05:50.120 --> 00:05:54.540
I feel like there's like a list of possible Dundees and they're all like so hilarious.

115
00:05:54.540 --> 00:05:58.600
We also wanted to know what it was like to direct Will Ferrell.

116
00:05:58.800 --> 00:05:59.220
Yes.

117
00:05:59.420 --> 00:05:59.740
Yeah.

118
00:06:00.100 --> 00:06:01.800
I mean, he's so funny.

119
00:06:02.020 --> 00:06:03.040
Here's what she had to say.

120
00:06:03.260 --> 00:06:07.380
You know, what's interesting was I remember Will Ferrell being incredibly kind.

121
00:06:07.880 --> 00:06:12.540
Like there's these photos taken from set of him kind of like looking to me like, hey, was this good?

122
00:06:12.680 --> 00:06:14.580
When he absolutely did not have to do this.

123
00:06:14.700 --> 00:06:17.760
It's not like D'Angelo Vickers was this like challenging character for him.

124
00:06:18.120 --> 00:06:19.820
You know, he was just doing his thing.

125
00:06:19.820 --> 00:06:28.740
But I thought it was really nice that he was looking to me for input, you know, occasionally, which I thought was great.

126
00:06:29.440 --> 00:06:30.320
He's the beSt.

127
00:06:30.540 --> 00:06:32.020
I saw those pictures, by the way.

128
00:06:32.380 --> 00:06:32.660
Yeah.

129
00:06:32.860 --> 00:06:33.060
Yeah.

130
00:06:33.060 --> 00:06:37.140
I went to NBC's website and found all these office photos and there they are.

131
00:06:37.300 --> 00:06:38.580
Like he's kind of looking over her shoulder.

132
00:06:38.700 --> 00:06:39.820
She's pointing to the script.

133
00:06:40.020 --> 00:06:42.960
I was like, oh, that's so like awesome that that was documented.

134
00:06:43.500 --> 00:06:44.260
Oh, my gosh.

135
00:06:44.260 --> 00:06:49.340
If I were her, I would have one of those framed in my house like 11 by 14.

136
00:06:49.740 --> 00:06:51.740
But you would still introduce yourself to him.

137
00:06:51.840 --> 00:06:52.640
I would still.

138
00:06:53.220 --> 00:06:56.000
You would hold up the picture and be like, well, remember I met.

139
00:06:56.280 --> 00:06:59.360
I'd get a wallet size just for that purpose to remind him.

140
00:06:59.360 --> 00:07:00.340
Wouldn't be creepy at all.

141
00:07:00.520 --> 00:07:02.080
Are you ready for fastback number two?

142
00:07:02.320 --> 00:07:02.720
Yeah.

143
00:07:03.400 --> 00:07:05.160
It's a location alert.

144
00:07:05.640 --> 00:07:06.400
Ding, ding, ding, ding.

145
00:07:06.680 --> 00:07:08.620
Were you wondering where we filmed the Dundies?

146
00:07:08.960 --> 00:07:09.940
Because I'll tell you.

147
00:07:10.120 --> 00:07:10.700
Please do.

148
00:07:10.880 --> 00:07:11.580
I remember.

149
00:07:11.880 --> 00:07:14.060
Well, I know you were there speaking to our listeners.

150
00:07:14.260 --> 00:07:15.800
I was trying a new thing.

151
00:07:16.660 --> 00:07:22.140
The Dundies are held at a fictitious restaurant called Louis Volpe, where the breadsticks apparently

152
00:07:22.140 --> 00:07:22.940
taste like crack.

153
00:07:23.360 --> 00:07:28.360
The exterior scenes when we're all out in the parking lot and the establishing shots were

154
00:07:28.360 --> 00:07:32.100
filmed at Baroni's Restaurant in Valley Glen, California.

155
00:07:33.000 --> 00:07:37.920
The craft services guys would often order pizza from Baroni's if we were working late on set.

156
00:07:38.080 --> 00:07:40.700
So this was kind of a nice little wink and nod.

157
00:07:40.920 --> 00:07:44.020
I remember my very favorite pizza that we would get from there.

158
00:07:44.260 --> 00:07:44.840
Which one?

159
00:07:45.020 --> 00:07:48.600
Well, first of all, it's all Neapolitan style and it's like a rectangle, right?

160
00:07:48.900 --> 00:07:50.460
What's Neapolitan style mean?

161
00:07:50.700 --> 00:07:51.220
Like thin?

162
00:07:51.580 --> 00:07:51.760
Yeah.

163
00:07:51.900 --> 00:07:52.020
Yeah.

164
00:07:52.200 --> 00:07:54.220
My favorite order is the house combination.

165
00:07:54.520 --> 00:07:55.300
What's on it?

166
00:07:55.520 --> 00:07:56.960
Sausage, pepperoni, and bell pepper.

167
00:07:57.200 --> 00:07:57.980
That sounds good.

168
00:07:57.980 --> 00:08:03.680
Well, I looked at their website and their pizza is a blend of recipes from six Italian families,

169
00:08:03.960 --> 00:08:10.620
all related, who created the original Baroni's famous Italian restaurant, which opened in 1945.

170
00:08:11.230 --> 00:08:13.480
It's a Los Angeles landmark and it's delicious.

171
00:08:13.820 --> 00:08:16.420
And they are famous for their rectangular pizza.

172
00:08:16.840 --> 00:08:19.260
Do you know why it's a rectangle?

173
00:08:19.620 --> 00:08:21.560
I found out on the website, Angela.

174
00:08:21.680 --> 00:08:22.620
I don't.

175
00:08:22.620 --> 00:08:28.320
They put it in a rectangle because their first pizza ovens were kind of tiny and this way

176
00:08:28.320 --> 00:08:29.900
they could fit more pizza in there.

177
00:08:30.260 --> 00:08:33.620
It was just a space saver thing.

178
00:08:34.340 --> 00:08:35.020
Efficiency.

179
00:08:35.020 --> 00:08:36.000
And it stuck.

180
00:08:36.260 --> 00:08:36.960
It did.

181
00:08:37.460 --> 00:08:42.200
Now, the interior for the Dundies was not filmed at Baroni's restaurant.

182
00:08:42.660 --> 00:08:45.880
It was a set that we built over on our warehouse stages.

183
00:08:46.780 --> 00:08:50.140
Randy even sent us a blueprint of Michael Gallenberg's design.

184
00:08:50.140 --> 00:08:51.440
It is so elaborate.

185
00:08:51.720 --> 00:08:52.140
I know.

186
00:08:52.640 --> 00:08:54.840
There's like the restaurant with all the tables.

187
00:08:55.000 --> 00:08:55.840
There's the hallways.

188
00:08:55.840 --> 00:08:59.480
There's a fakie bathroom where D'Angelo's going to have to throw up.

189
00:08:59.640 --> 00:09:00.900
There's the host stand.

190
00:09:01.200 --> 00:09:02.300
He built a whole restaurant.

191
00:09:02.900 --> 00:09:09.900
And I remember walking through the fakie hallway into the restaurant and it's so mind-blowing.

192
00:09:10.060 --> 00:09:12.220
I don't even know how to explain to people what it's like.

193
00:09:12.600 --> 00:09:16.340
I shot a show splitting up together that had like a whole house.

194
00:09:16.340 --> 00:09:20.500
And when you were in the stage, you felt like you were in a real house.

195
00:09:21.000 --> 00:09:23.900
And then you would walk out the door and you were just in a warehouse.

196
00:09:24.120 --> 00:09:25.540
It's very mind-bendy.

197
00:09:25.800 --> 00:09:29.820
But the craziest thing about that house was that there were stairs to go upstairs,

198
00:09:29.820 --> 00:09:31.280
but they didn't go anywhere.

199
00:09:31.480 --> 00:09:31.680
Yeah.

200
00:09:32.260 --> 00:09:33.580
It was just like a landing.

201
00:09:33.780 --> 00:09:38.640
And then we would have to get in a golf cart and drive all the way over to a different warehouse

202
00:09:38.640 --> 00:09:42.360
on the lot where the upstairs stage was built.

203
00:09:42.600 --> 00:09:46.760
Very similar to, I guess, when we would have to do our elevator stuff on the office.

204
00:09:46.780 --> 00:09:48.900
We'd have to relocate to a different building.

205
00:09:49.100 --> 00:09:51.500
That's one of my favorite things about sets.

206
00:09:51.740 --> 00:09:55.840
And when I watch shows now like Friends or, you know, anything like that,

207
00:09:55.980 --> 00:09:58.200
I think about like, oh, when they go up their stairs,

208
00:09:58.360 --> 00:10:01.200
I wonder if it was like us where we went upstairs and then just nothing.

209
00:10:01.500 --> 00:10:04.060
You would just like maybe just fall off a ladder

210
00:10:04.060 --> 00:10:07.380
or be wedged in a little tiny corner and you couldn't move.

211
00:10:07.560 --> 00:10:09.940
And I think about like, you know,

212
00:10:10.060 --> 00:10:13.180
Jennifer Aniston and Courtney Cox up there wedged in a corner.

213
00:10:13.420 --> 00:10:14.000
I don't know.

214
00:10:14.160 --> 00:10:15.380
I'm re-watching Friends, guys.

215
00:10:15.560 --> 00:10:16.500
I'm thinking about this now.

216
00:10:16.780 --> 00:10:18.200
You started from the beginning.

217
00:10:18.520 --> 00:10:18.820
Yeah.

218
00:10:19.140 --> 00:10:20.080
How far are you?

219
00:10:20.200 --> 00:10:21.200
We're almost done.

220
00:10:21.560 --> 00:10:22.040
Wow.

221
00:10:22.260 --> 00:10:22.480
Yeah.

222
00:10:23.220 --> 00:10:23.700
Wow.

223
00:10:25.260 --> 00:10:26.660
Follow up to Office, ladies.

224
00:10:26.940 --> 00:10:29.020
Would it be weird if we re-watched Friends?

225
00:10:29.020 --> 00:10:29.940
We weren't on it.

226
00:10:30.700 --> 00:10:31.180
Maybe.

227
00:10:34.060 --> 00:10:34.520
Just a thought.

228
00:10:35.020 --> 00:10:35.360
All right.

229
00:10:36.000 --> 00:10:37.500
Last part of this fast fact.

230
00:10:37.680 --> 00:10:40.300
Do you remember the website we talked about before?

231
00:10:40.480 --> 00:10:43.500
It's called I Am Not a Stalker with this gal, Lindsay,

232
00:10:43.800 --> 00:10:47.820
who visits the locations of places made famous on TV or in movies?

233
00:10:48.160 --> 00:10:49.200
Yes, I've seen her website.

234
00:10:49.440 --> 00:10:49.780
Yes.

235
00:10:49.880 --> 00:10:50.640
It's fascinating.

236
00:10:51.740 --> 00:10:55.280
Well, she did a really fun blog where she went to Baroni's

237
00:10:55.280 --> 00:10:58.740
and she took pictures of the interior of the real Baroni's.

238
00:10:58.780 --> 00:11:01.300
So if you want to see it, it's all on her blog.

239
00:11:01.300 --> 00:11:06.900
And while we did not film there, the interior of the restaurant is featured in the movies

240
00:11:06.900 --> 00:11:10.960
Fast Times at Ridgemont High and the Billy Bob Thornton movie Bad News Bears.

241
00:11:12.240 --> 00:11:17.180
If you go there and you go to the bathroom and you walk through that one big part of the

242
00:11:17.180 --> 00:11:19.900
restaurant they hardly ever use, you know, to get to the bathroom.

243
00:11:19.900 --> 00:11:21.280
I've never eaten there in person.

244
00:11:21.480 --> 00:11:21.800
What?

245
00:11:22.000 --> 00:11:23.380
I've never eaten there in person.

246
00:11:23.520 --> 00:11:25.000
We need a BFF date there.

247
